info.description:
arcgate is a checkpoint between your agent and the DEXes on Arc. It finds the token you meant, the venues that can actually fill it, and whether the trade is safe, then prices it and hands back unsigned transactions. **It never holds funds and never signs.** Your wallet does both.

# A checkpoint, not a DEX

Three paid calls, in this order, then your wallet takes over, and a free receipt tells you whether it landed.

<ol>
<li><code>search</code><strong>Right token.</strong><span>Every contract with the name you asked for, ranked, with impostors flagged by identity. Pick the address you meant.</span></li>
<li><code>quote</code><strong>Right venue, safe to trade, best price.</strong><span>Only pools that exist and hold liquidity at this block. A simulated buy and sell-back from a fresh wallet. Prices at parity with KyberSwap. Returns a quoteId that is good for 120 seconds. Name your <code>taker</code> and it also says whether that wallet can finish the trade, and what finishing costs (<code>readiness</code>).</span></li>
<li><code>swap</code><strong>Unsigned transactions.</strong><span>The quoteId and your taker address become the transactions to send, any approvals first, then the swap, re-simulated from your wallet first, and executed through arcgate's own ArcgateRouter contract. Approvals your allowance already covers are skipped, and a route funded entirely by native USDC needs no approval or signature at all. With the default Permit2 approval you may otherwise get a permit to sign; the response carries `signatures[]` (typed data) and `next: "sign_permit"`. After signing the permit, the next step is `POST /trade/v1/swap/tx`.</span></li>
<li><code>swap/tx</code><strong>Free, for signed permits.</strong><span>FREE, never payment-gated. Turns a signed Permit2 permit into the final swap transactions. Body: `{ quoteId, taker, recipient? (defaults to taker), deadlineSec?, permit: { message, signature } }`. Permit required; no `approval` field. Returns the same 200 body as a swap answer with no pending signature. Only works right after a paid /swap for the same quote, taker and recipient that asked for a permit, and only once. Otherwise 409 `no_pending_swap` (next: `fix_request`). An expired quote answers 410 `quote_expired`, without a fresh quote.</span></li>
<li><code>sign</code><strong>Your wallet signs.</strong><span>And broadcasts. arcgate never does.</span></li>
<li><code>receipt</code><strong>Did it land?</strong><span>Free. Send the quoteId and your transaction hashes; get back what you received against minAmountOut, and pass or fail.</span></li>
</ol>

# Paying arcgate from your own code

`search`, `quote` and `swap` are paid per call with [x402](https://x402.org) v2, in USDC on Arc. No API keys, no accounts.

1. Call the endpoint with no payment. The answer is `402 Payment Required`, with the requirements (scheme, network, amount, asset, `payTo`, timeout) base64-encoded in the `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header.
2. Sign an EIP-3009 authorization for that amount and retry the **same** request with the signed payload, base64-encoded, in `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE`.
3. A paid response carries the settlement receipt in `PAYMENT-RESPONSE`.

Any x402 client runs that loop for you. Fees are paid in **real USDC on Arc mainnet**, the same chain the trade runs on. Arc USDC isn't one of x402's built-in assets (the standard client libraries ship USDC for Base only), so the client must allow it explicitly, or every payment is refused before it's signed. In JavaScript, that's `spendControls.allowedAssets` with network `eip155:5042`, asset `0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000` and a per-payment maximum, as in the example below. Install once:

```bash
npm install @x402/fetch @x402/core @x402/evm viem
# Python: pip install "x402[evm,requests]"
```

The example follows the language picked under **Client Libraries**. Its first call is one paid `search` (0.005 USDC), which proves the setup; the calls after it are the whole trade:

```js
import { wrapFetchWithPayment } from "@x402/fetch";
import { x402Client } from "@x402/core/client";
import { ExactEvmScheme } from "@x402/evm/exact/client";
import { privateKeyToAccount } from "viem/accounts";

// The server shown on this page.
const API = process.env.API_URL;
// Holds USDC on Arc mainnet: fees are real.
const signer =
  privateKeyToAccount(process.env.PRIVATE_KEY);
// The address you picked from search.
const TOKEN = "0x…";

// Fees are paid on Arc mainnet. (A local dev stack
// charges on Arc testnet: "eip155:5042002".)
const FEE_NETWORK = "eip155:5042";
const USDC =
  "0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000";

const x402 = x402Client.fromConfig({
  schemes: [{
    network: FEE_NETWORK,
    client: new ExactEvmScheme(signer),
  }],
  // Allow Arc USDC, capped per payment at the top
  // swap fee (5 USDC).
  spendControls: {
    allowedAssets: [{
      network: FEE_NETWORK,
      asset: USDC,
      maxAmountPerPayment: "5000000",
    }],
  },
});
const pay = wrapFetchWithPayment(fetch, x402);

async function call(path, body) {
  const res = await pay(`${API}/trade/v1/${path}`, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify(body),
  });
  const json = await res.json();
  // Errors are never charged.
  if (!res.ok) {
    const code = json.error?.code;
    throw new Error(`${path}: ${res.status} ${code}`);
  }
  return json;
}

// 0.005 USDC: the first paid call. If this returns
// results, Arc USDC is allowed and the wallet pays.
const found = await call("search", { query: "USDC" });

// 0.01 USDC: route, round-trip loss, 120 second
// expiry. Sells 100 USDC.
const quote = await call("quote", {
  sell: USDC, buy: TOKEN, amount: "100",
});

// 0.01 to 5 USDC by size. approval "approve" is
// always one call: at most one exact-amount ERC-20
// approve(ArcgateRouter, amountIn), no Permit2, no
// signature. The default, "permit2", can return a
// permit to sign. Then POST /trade/v1/swap/tx (free)
// with the signed permit turns it into transactions.
const swap = await call("swap", {
  quoteId: quote.quoteId,
  taker: signer.address,
  approval: "approve",
});

// swap.transactions: approvals still needed, then
// the swap, all unsigned. Read what you sign. Your
// wallet signs and sends each one, in order.
```

```python
# pip install "x402[evm,requests]"
import os
from eth_account import Account
from x402 import (
    SchemeRegistration,
    x402ClientConfig,
    x402ClientSync,
)
from x402.http.clients import x402_requests
from x402.mechanisms.evm.exact import (
    ExactEvmClientScheme)

# The server shown on this page.
API = os.environ["API_URL"]
# Holds USDC on Arc mainnet: fees are real.
account = Account.from_key(os.environ["PRIVATE_KEY"])
# The address you picked from search.
TOKEN = "0x…"

# Fees are paid on Arc mainnet. (A local dev stack
# charges on Arc testnet: "eip155:5042002".)
FEE_NETWORK = "eip155:5042"
USDC = "0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000"

x402 = x402ClientSync.from_config(x402ClientConfig(
    schemes=[SchemeRegistration(
        network=FEE_NETWORK,
        client=ExactEvmClientScheme(account),
    )],
    # Allow Arc USDC, capped per payment at the top
    # swap fee (5 USDC).
    spend_controls={"allowed_assets": [{
        "network": FEE_NETWORK,
        "asset": USDC,
        "max_amount_per_payment": "5000000",
    }]},
))
session = x402_requests(x402)

def call(path, body):
    url = f"{API}/trade/v1/{path}"
    res = session.post(url, json=body)
    data = res.json()
    # Errors are never charged.
    if not res.ok:
        code = data["error"]["code"]
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"{path}: {res.status_code} {code}")
    return data

# 0.005 USDC: the first paid call. If this returns
# results, Arc USDC is allowed and the wallet pays.
found = call("search", {"query": "USDC"})

# 0.01 USDC: route, round-trip loss, 120 second
# expiry. Sells 100 USDC.
quote = call("quote", {
    "sell": USDC, "buy": TOKEN, "amount": "100",
})

# 0.01 to 5 USDC by size. approval "approve" is
# always one call: at most one exact-amount approve,
# no Permit2, no signature.
swap = call("swap", {
    "quoteId": quote["quoteId"],
    "taker": account.address,
    "approval": "approve",
})

# swap["transactions"]: approvals still needed, then
# the swap, all unsigned. Read what you sign. Your
# wallet signs and sends each one, in order.
```

```bash
# Free calls work from curl as they are.
curl "$API_URL/trade/v1/venues"
curl "$API_URL/health"

# A paid call answers 402, with its price in
# PAYMENT-REQUIRED (base64 JSON).
USDC=0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000
TOKEN=0x…   # the address you picked from search
BODY="{\"sell\":\"$USDC\",\"buy\":\"$TOKEN\","
BODY+="\"amount\":\"100\"}"
curl -s -D - -o /dev/null \
  -X POST "$API_URL/trade/v1/quote" \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' -d "$BODY" \
  | grep -i '^payment-required' | cut -d' ' -f2 \
  | tr -d '\r' | base64 -d

# curl can't sign the EIP-3009 payment for the
# retry. Pay with the JavaScript or Python client
# (pick it under Client Libraries), or connect over
# MCP below.
```

# Prices

| Operation | USDC per call |
| --- | --- |
| `search` | 0.005 |
| `quote` | 0.01 |
| `swap` under 1k | 0.01 |
| `swap` 1k to 10k | 0.05 |
| `swap` over 10k | 0.05 + 0.5 bps above 10k, capped at 5 |
| `swap/tx`, `venues`, `health`, `openapi`, `receipt` | free |

Errors are never charged. A `cannot_sell` safety verdict on a quote is an answer, so it is charged. `insufficient_liquidity` (`422`) and an expired quote (`410`) are free. Nothing is taken from your swap output. `swap` is paid once per quote. When it requests a signature, `swap/tx` turns the signed permit into the transactions, free.

# Calling the API

Every operation has a **Test Request** button that calls the API from this page. Free operations, `venues` and `health`, just run. A paid one answers `402`, and the page pays it with your browser wallet (MetaMask, or any EIP-1193 wallet): it switches the wallet to Arc mainnet, asks you to sign the USDC authorization for the price shown, and resends the call. An MCP tool call on `POST /trade/v1/mcp` is paid the same way: when the tool answers with its price, the page signs it and resends the call with the payment inside.

> [!CAUTION]
> **Test Requests on paid operations cost real money.** Each one is a real x402 payment in USDC on Arc mainnet, at the prices above, from the wallet you connect. Your wallet shows the exact amount before you sign, and nothing is paid unless you sign. A single payment is never allowed above 5 USDC, the top swap fee, and errors are never charged.

`swap` takes a `quoteId`, so run `quote` first and paste its id into the swap body. The `swap` answer is unsigned transactions for Arc mainnet: this page shows them, it never sends them.

From your own code, use an x402 client as above, or the MCP server below.

Every error body has `next` beside `error`, saying what to do: `requote`, `retry` (after `retryAfterSec` when given), `fix_request`, `stop` or `pay`; a 200 that needs an action has one too.

Every response carries an `X-Request-Id` header. If a call goes wrong in a way you don't expect, send us that id with your report: it finds that exact call in our logs, including what the payment facilitator said about your payment.

# MCP for agents

`POST /trade/v1/mcp` is a [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) server over Streamable HTTP. It is stateless: there's no session to open or keep, and every call is a single JSON-RPC `POST` (send `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream`).

| Tool | What it does | USDC per call |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `tradeSearch` | Resolves a ticker, name or address to tokens | 0.005 |
| `tradeQuote` | Prices a swap and returns a `quoteId` | 0.01 |
| `tradeSwap` | Turns a `quoteId` into unsigned transactions | 0.01 to 5, by size |
| `tradeSwapTx` | Turns a signed Permit2 permit into final swap transactions | free |
| `tradeVenues` | Lists venues, hubs and launchpads | free |
| `health` | Reports service status | free |

Each tool takes the same input as its operation on this page and costs the same. Payments are real USDC on Arc mainnet.

**Paying inside MCP.** MCP has no HTTP headers, so x402 rides in the tool call itself. A paid tool called without payment answers `isError: true` with the price in `structuredContent`: the same requirements a `402` carries. An x402-aware client signs them and calls again with the payment in `params._meta["x402/payment"]`, and the result carries the settlement receipt in `_meta["x402/payment-response"]`. A result with `isError: true` is never charged. `@x402/mcp` runs that loop for you:

```js
import { createx402MCPClient } from "@x402/mcp";
import { ExactEvmScheme } from "@x402/evm/exact/client";
import { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from
  "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/streamableHttp.js";
import { privateKeyToAccount } from "viem/accounts";

// Holds USDC on Arc mainnet: fees are real.
const signer =
  privateKeyToAccount(process.env.PRIVATE_KEY);
// The address you picked from tradeSearch.
const TOKEN = "0x…";

const FEE_NETWORK = "eip155:5042";
const USDC =
  "0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000";

const client = createx402MCPClient({
  name: "my-agent",
  version: "1.0.0",
  schemes: [{
    network: FEE_NETWORK,
    client: new ExactEvmScheme(signer),
  }],
  // Allow Arc USDC, capped per payment at the top
  // swap fee (5 USDC).
  spendControls: {
    allowedAssets: [{
      network: FEE_NETWORK,
      asset: USDC,
      maxAmountPerPayment: "5000000",
    }],
  },
});
const url = new URL(
  `${process.env.API_URL}/trade/v1/mcp`);
await client.connect(
  new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(url));
// A tool's answer is JSON in its text content.
const body = (result) =>
  JSON.parse(result.content[0].text);

// Paid tools pay on their own; free ones never do.
const quote = await client.callTool("tradeQuote", {
  sell: USDC, buy: TOKEN, amount: "100",
});
if (quote.isError) {
  throw new Error(JSON.stringify(quote.content));
}
// quote.paymentResponse is the settlement receipt.
const { quoteId } = body(quote);

const swap = await client.callTool("tradeSwap", {
  quoteId, taker: signer.address, approval: "approve",
});
// body(swap).transactions: unsigned, in order. Read
// what you sign, then sign and send.
```

A generic MCP host (Claude Desktop, Cursor and the like) can connect and call the free tools. Its paid calls come back with the price, because paying takes an x402-aware client like the one above.

# Agent skill

`arcgate-trade` is an [Agent Skill](https://agentskills.io) that teaches an agent to take a plain-English order ("buy 100 USDC of X", "sell my MOLLY") through search, quote and swap: settling the trade's direction, resolving tickers to addresses, reading the safety verdict, and handing the user's wallet the transactions to sign. It includes a script that makes the paid calls with a wallet key, capped per payment.

**Claude Code:**

```bash
/plugin marketplace add arcgate-dev/docs
/plugin install arcgate-trade@arcgate
```

**Any other agent:** load [`/plugin/skills/arcgate-trade/SKILL.md`](/plugin/skills/arcgate-trade/SKILL.md) from this site; the script is in the same folder.

Paid calls need an agent that can run code and a wallet key in `PRIVATE_KEY`, holding USDC on Arc. The fees are real. An agent that can't run code can still plan the trade and make the free calls.
